BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:innovate-uk-next-wave-createch-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260811
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260812
SUMMARY:Deadline: Innovate UK: Next Wave Breakthrough Wave 1\, Createch (20
 26)
DESCRIPTION:Next Wave Breakthrough Wave 1 is Innovate UK's current open Cre
 atech competition\, investing up to GBP 10 million in early-stage innovati
 on at the intersection of creative technology and the frontier industries 
 named in the Industrial Strategy: video games\, advertising and marketing\
 , film and television\, and music and the performing and visual arts. It f
 unds industrial research to take innovations beyond proof of concept and d
 e-risk future investment\, with individual projects between GBP 100\,000 a
 nd 500\,000 in total costs. Grant rates depend on organisation size\, from
  70 percent for micro and small enterprises down to 50 percent for large o
 rganisations\, so applicants must supply match funding for the remainder. 
 The lead must be a UK-registered SME\; non-profits and CICs are barred fro
 m leading\, though they can participate as collaborators alongside a comme
 rcial lead. Non-funded partners may be based internationally. The competit
 ion opened 23 June 2026 and closes 11 August 2026 at 11:00am UK time. NOTE
  ON FIT: this is an R&D competition for creative-technology product develo
 pment\, not a fund for delivering festivals or exhibitions\, so it suits a
 n organisation whose work can be framed as industrial research with a comm
 ercial SME lead.\n\nOrganisation: Innovate UK\, UK Research and Innovation
 \nLocation: United Kingdom. Project work must be carried out in the UK\, r
 esults exploited in the UK\, and most funding spent in the UK.\nAward: GBP
  100\,000 to 500\,000 total project costs from a GBP 10 million fund\; no 
 organisation may receive more than GBP 3 million across the programme. Gra
 nt intervention rates for industrial research: up to 70 percent for micro 
 and small enterprises\, 60 percent for medium\, 50 percent for large. Rese
 arch organisations doing non-economic work can claim up to 100 percent\, o
 r 80 percent of full economic cost for academic institutions. The balance 
 is match funding. No application fee.\nDuration: 9 to 12 months\; projects
  must start by 1 January 2027 and end by 31 December 2027.\n\nMore: https:
 //apply-for-innovation-funding.service.gov.uk/competition/2517/overview/6f
 f2066d-4639-44e6-bb4f-ce7099222739?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply-for-innovation-funding.service.gov.uk/competition/2517/ov
 erview/6ff2066d-4639-44e6-bb4f-ce7099222739?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United Kingdom. Project work must be carried out in the UK\, resul
 ts exploited in the UK\, and most funding spent in the UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lse-digipulse-research-officer-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260722
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260723
SUMMARY:Deadline: LSE Research Officer: DigiPulse\, Digital Futures for Chi
 ldren Centre
DESCRIPTION:DigiPulse is a three-year longitudinal\, international research
  project in LSE's Digital Futures for Children centre investigating the re
 al-time relationships between children's smartphone use and their wellbein
 g\, with data collected in the UK\, Brazil and Kenya. The project combines
  passive smartphone tracking\, Ecological Momentary Assessment (EMA) and l
 ongitudinal surveys with around 750 children and young people aged 10-17. 
 THE ROLE: the Research Officer plays a central part in project management\
 , development\, data collection and analysis\, with particular responsibil
 ity for developing and refining EMA instruments\, contributing to the rese
 arch literature and hypotheses\, supporting participatory and consultative
  work with children\, and conducting statistical analysis and producing fi
 ndings\, working closely with partners in the UK\, Brazil and Kenya. REQUI
 REMENTS: a completed PhD in a relevant discipline (e.g. media and communic
 ations\, psychology\, psychiatry\, sociology\, education\, or computationa
 l/data social science)\; experience developing surveys or questionnaire in
 struments\; experience designing or conducting longitudinal research studi
 es\; and experience with EMA\, experience-sampling or other intensive long
 itudinal data collection methods. APPLY via jobs.lse.ac.uk\; questions to 
 k.g.ringmar-sylwander@lse.ac.uk. Closing date 22 July 2026 (23:59 UK time)
 \; late applications are not accepted.\n\nOrganisation: London School of E
 conomics and Political Science (LSE)\, Department of Media and Communicati
 ons\nLocation: London\, UK (hybrid working)\nAward: GBP 43\,277 to 51\,714
  per year inclusive of London allowance\, with potential to progress to GB
 P 55\,497. Full-time (1.0 FTE)\, funded until December 2028. Benefits: occ
 upational pension scheme\, generous annual leave\, hybrid working\, traini
 ng and development. No application fee.\nDuration: Full-time fixed-term po
 st\, funded until December 2028\n\nMore: https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/
 W/2504/0/471333/15539/research-officer-digipulse-digital-futures-for-child
 ren-centre/Referral?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/2504/0/471333/15539/research-officer
 -digipulse-digital-futures-for-children-centre/Referral?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK (hybrid working)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aria-future-proofing-climate-weather-opportunity-seeds-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: ARIA: Future Proofing Our Climate and Weather (Opportunit
 y Seeds) 2026
DESCRIPTION:ARIA is seeking proposals for opportunity seeds\, funding of up
  to GBP 500\,000 for early-stage research that explores new pathways for c
 limate adaptation and resilience. Decarbonisation is the only sustainable 
 route out of the climate crisis\, but environmental changes are outpacing 
 current mitigation efforts\, and if an abrupt alteration in a climate syst
 em were to unfold we would have no tools to mitigate the effects\; scienti
 fic\, engineering and social science research could provide practical and 
 responsible intervention and adaptation options. WHAT THEY ARE LOOKING FOR
 : ideas ranging from early-stage\, curiosity-driven research through to ad
 vanced translational science. Specific areas of interest include\, but are
  not limited to: carbon dioxide removal (novel concepts to accelerate the 
 drawdown of atmospheric carbon)\; extreme weather forecasting (breakthroug
 hs in predicting extreme weather events to better prepare vulnerable syste
 ms)\; and new ideas related to solar geoengineering\, strictly excluding p
 roposals specifically designed to investigate altering the Earth's surface
  temperature by affecting planetary albedo\, incoming solar radiation\, or
  the atmosphere's emissivity (the core focus of the main Exploring Climate
  Cooling\, ECC\, programme). OUT OF SCOPE: ideas that squarely fit within 
 the ECC programme\; emissions reduction projects and improvements to the e
 nergy efficiency of buildings or vehicles\; and commercial products or res
 earch likely to be funded by traditional venture capital or grants. CONSTR
 AINTS: any proposal for outdoor experiments must demonstrate conformity to
  ARIA's governance framework for outdoor experiments\, and all successful 
 applicants must sign ARIA's Intellectual Property Pledge to ensure finding
 s are accessible for the public good. Application deadline: 31 July 2026\,
  14:00 BST.\n\nOrganisation: ARIA (Advanced Research and Invention Agency)
 \nLocation: UK-based funder (ARIA)\; proposals welcome across scientific\,
  engineering and social science research.\nAward: Opportunity seeds of up 
 to GBP 500\,000 for early-stage research. No application fee.\nDuration: E
 arly-stage research projects (project length set by the proposal)\n\nMore:
  https://aria.org.uk/opportunity-spaces/future-proofing-our-climate-and-we
 ather/opportunity-seeds?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://aria.org.uk/opportunity-spaces/future-proofing-our-climate-and-
 weather/opportunity-seeds?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
LOCATION:UK-based funder (ARIA)\; proposals welcome across scientific\, eng
 ineering and social science research.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:british-council-iash-90th-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: British Council 90th Anniversary Research Fellowships at 
 IASH 2027 (Edinburgh)
DESCRIPTION:Twelve-month postdoctoral fellowship from the Institute for Adv
 anced Studies in the Humanities (IASH) at the University of Edinburgh\, in
  partnership with the British Council\, marking the Council's 90th anniver
 sary. Up to two fellowships per year as part of the 2025-2027 partnership.
  Fellows spend ten months residential at IASH (January to October 2027) fo
 llowed by up to two months of knowledge-exchange and dissemination work in
  their home country in collaboration with the British Council. ELIGIBILITY
 : postdoctoral researchers based in an ODA-recipient country where the Bri
 tish Council operates (full list of ~60+ countries published on the IASH p
 age\, spanning Albania to Zimbabwe and including the Occupied Palestinian 
 Territories)\; PhD completed within the last seven years (career breaks ex
 cluded from the seven-year window)\; applicants must not hold a permanent 
 university position and must not have held a prior IASH Fellowship. Resear
 ch themes should align with British Council priorities across Arts\, Educa
 tion and English language\, plus cross-cutting interests in international 
 relations\, soft power\, international development\, peace building\, and 
 cultural relations and diplomacy. NOTE: applicants are required to contact
  relevant University of Edinburgh researchers before submitting\; informat
 ional webinar 26 May 2026. Decisions communicated late September 2026. Ref
 erences (minimum two\, maximum three) must be emailed by referees directly
  to iash@ed.ac.uk by the deadline.\n\nOrganisation: Institute for Advanced
  Studies in the Humanities (IASH)\, University of Edinburgh\, in partnersh
 ip with the British Council\nLocation: Edinburgh\, UK (10 months residenti
 al at IASH\; final 2 months in home country)\nAward: GBP 2\,500 per month 
 bursary for 12 months\, plus travel expenses\, dedicated office space\, Un
 iversity of Edinburgh email and library access\, a University mentor\, wee
 kly Fellows' Lunch\, work-in-progress seminars\, full calendar of Institut
 e and College events\, and opportunities to participate in or design funde
 d workshops and colloquia. No application fee.\nDuration: 12 months (10 mo
 nths residential at IASH\, January to October 2027\; followed by November-
 December 2027 knowledge-exchange in the home country)\n\nMore: https://www
 .iash.ed.ac.uk/british-council-90th-anniversary-research-fellowships?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iash.ed.ac.uk/british-council-90th-anniversary-research-fel
 lowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Edinburgh\, UK (10 months residential at IASH\; final 2 months in 
 home country)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:govai-uk-winter-fellowship-2027-research-track@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: GovAI UK Winter Fellowship 2027 - Research Track
DESCRIPTION:GovAI's UK Winter Fellowship (Research Track) is a three-month 
 in-person fellowship in London for researchers and policy professionals se
 eking to enter or advance careers in AI governance and policy. Fellows con
 duct independent research on a topic of their choice\, paired with a GovAI
  supervisor from the team or network\; the first two weeks are spent explo
 ring the AI governance landscape before a research proposal is finalised. 
 Research outputs may include a report\, white paper\, journal article\, op
 -ed\, or blog post targeted at an audience relevant to AI governance. Alon
 gside the project\, fellows attend expert Q&As\, weekly seminars\, skills 
 workshops\, work-in-progress meetings\, and social events\, and are encour
 aged to discuss follow-on career opportunities with GovAI's team and netwo
 rk. GovAI alumni have gone on to roles in US\, UK\, and EU government\; le
 ading AI companies (Anthropic\, DeepMind\, OpenAI)\; top think tanks (CSET
 \, RAND)\; and leading universities (Oxford\, Cambridge). AMOUNT: £12\,00
 0 salary for three months\, plus travel support to London and weekday lunc
 h. Visa sponsorship for a 3-month temporary work visa is available\; succe
 ssful visa applicants must remain in their country of application while th
 e visa is processed. ELIGIBILITY: open to a wide range of backgrounds (gov
 ernment\, academia\, industry\, civil society)\; no strict degree requirem
 ents\; relevant areas include public policy\, political science\, computer
  science\, economics\, biosecurity\, cybersecurity\, China studies\, and r
 isk management. Senior candidates who need accommodations should email fel
 lowships@governance.ai with subject line 'senior candidate winter fellowsh
 ip 2027' and a CV. SELECTION CRITERIA: quality and clarity of written work
 \, relevant expertise\, judgement in prioritising research directions\, an
 d team fit (intellectual honesty\, openness to feedback\, comfort with unc
 ertainty). APPLICATION PROCESS: (1) written application and automated abst
 ract reasoning assessment\; (2) paid remote work test (August 2026)\; (3) 
 remote interview and reference checks (September 2026)\; decisions communi
 cated October 2026. Deadline: 12 July 2026\, 23:59 BST.\n\nOrganisation: C
 entre for the Governance of AI (GovAI)\nLocation: London\, UK\; in-person\
 , full-time.\nAward: £12\,000 salary for the three-month term\, plus trav
 el support to London and weekday lunch provided. Visa sponsorship (3-month
  temporary work visa) available. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 months\,
  18 January to 9 April 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.governance.ai/post/winte
 r-fellowship-2027-research-track?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://www.governance.ai/post/winter-fellowship-2027-research-track?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK\; in-person\, full-time.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:waag-trust-me-im-an-airtist-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Trust me\, I'm an AI/rtist: Open Call for Creative Interv
 entions
DESCRIPTION:Waag Futurelab invites artists\, theatre makers\, choreographer
 s\, dancers\, game designers and other creative practitioners to engage di
 rectly with seven experimental generative AI Enablers being co-created wit
 h technologists and dance\, theatre and gaming professionals. The call is 
 an invitation into the development process: selected artists get access to
  the technologies\, datasets\, interfaces and assumptions that shape these
  systems while they are still being built. Rather than testing whether the
  systems work\, artists are asked to interrogate why they work the way the
 y do\, who they are designed for\, what forms of creativity they enable or
  constrain\, and what assumptions about culture\, intelligence\, authorshi
 p\, labour\, bodies\, audiences and society are embedded in them. Waag is 
 looking for individuals and collectives willing to move beyond adoption in
 to investigation\, and welcomes proposals that are speculative\, critical\
 , poetic\, provocative\, humorous\, performative\, constructive or disrupt
 ive\, including work that challenges\, misuses\, reimagines\, hacks\, subv
 erts or plays with the tools\, exposes hidden biases\, questions environme
 ntal costs\, or imagines alternative technological futures. THE SEVEN AI E
 NABLERS\, each requiring an on-site visit to its technology partner: the G
 eneric Procedural Content Generation Enabler (text-to-3D assets and avatar
 s\, CERTH\, Greece)\; the Data-Driven Cultural Content Adaptation Enabler 
 (audience-aware creative content ideation with ranked candidate ideas\, CE
 RTH\, Greece)\; the Cross-Domain Audience Engagement Analytics Enabler (tu
 rning heterogeneous cultural participation data into interpretable engagem
 ent signals\, The Audience Agency\, Warrington\, UK)\; the Dance Style Ana
 lysis and Generation Enabler (dance video into virtual 3D choreography\, d
 issected into body\, effort\, shape and space components\, Centre of Excel
 lence for Research & Innovation\, Cyprus)\; the Immersive Design Integrati
 on Enabler for Dance & Theatre (NLP-driven XR environments with speech-to-
 3D scene editing\, Cyprus)\; the Music-to-Movement Translation Enabler (au
 dio into a time-aligned feature set spanning genre classification\, emotio
 nal valence-arousal analysis\, rhythm extraction and latent-space projecti
 on\, feeding motion generation\, spatial audio and visual choreography\, A
 ristotle University of Thessaloniki\, Greece)\; and the Adaptive Narrative
  Enabler for Games & Theatre (an evolutionary AI trained on creator-writte
 n story elements that generates infinite story variations\, explicitly rej
 ecting LLMs because of their dependence on copyrighted or public-domain co
 rpora that encode 19th and early 20th century sexism\, racism and imperial
 ism\, CAUSA Creation\, Austria). ELIGIBILITY: artists\, designers\, creati
 ve technologists\, theatre makers\, dancers\, choreographers and game desi
 gners\, as individuals or collectives\; applicants must hold a European VA
 T number. An info session with live demonstrations of the AI Enablers runs
  31 July 2026\, 10:00 to 11:30 CEST (meeting ID 995 7016 2341). Applicatio
 ns go through Homerun using the official Application Template\, uploaded a
 s a PDF\; read the Guide for Applicants\, which contains the full Enabler 
 descriptions\, before applying. Questions: zoenie at waag dot org. The AI 
 Enablers and this call are part of the Horizon Europe project HAMLET\, whi
 ch aims to make generative AI accessible to cultural organisations of all 
 sizes. Closes 30 September 2026\, 23:59 CEST. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Waag Futurelab (Horizon Europe project HAMLET)\nLocation: Europe. Enga
 ging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site visit to its technolog
 y partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warrington\, UK)\, the Ce
 ntre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\, Aristotle Universi
 ty of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austria). Final presentat
 ions September 2027.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 commission fee excluding VAT\, in
 clusive of artist fee\, travel\, accommodation and production costs. Appli
 cants must have a VAT number in Europe. No application fee.\nDuration: Jan
 uary to September 2027\, with final presentations in September 2027\n\nMor
 e: https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe. Engaging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site vi
 sit to its technology partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warri
 ngton\, UK)\, the Centre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\
 , Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austr
 ia). Final presentations September 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uea-deputy-dean-digital-creativity-institute-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: University of East Anglia: Deputy Dean\, CUCN-UEA Digital
  Creativity Institute
DESCRIPTION:Senior leadership post at the Digital Creativity Institute\, th
 e joint venture between the Communication University of China Nanjing (CUC
 N) and UEA\, sitting in UEA's Faculty of Arts and Humanities. The Deputy D
 ean leads UEA's academic contribution to the Institute\, covering curricul
 um and programme development\, staffing and quality assurance across digit
 al media\, creative technology and related disciplines\, and works with th
 e CUCN partner leadership on strategy\, recruitment and partnership growth
 . This is a rare senior role for someone who works across creative practic
 e and digital technology rather than in a single discipline\, and it invol
 ves regular engagement with the partner institution in China. Salary GBP 6
 9\,488 to 73\,709. Advertised 3 August 2026 through jobs.ac.uk and the UEA
  vacancy portal\; closes 31 August 2026. Check the UEA posting for the ful
 l person specification and whether the role requires periods based in Nanj
 ing.\n\nOrganisation: University of East Anglia (UEA)\, Faculty of Arts an
 d Humanities\nLocation: Norwich\, United Kingdom\nAward: GBP 69\,488 to 73
 \,709 per annum depending on qualifications and experience\, plus the stan
 dard UEA pension and leave package. No application fee.\nDuration: Senior 
 academic leadership appointment\; the post leads UEA's side of the joint C
 UCN-UEA Digital Creativity Institute\n\nMore: https://vacancies.uea.ac.uk/
 vacancies/vacancy-search-results.aspx?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://vacancies.uea.ac.uk/vacancies/vacancy-search-results.aspx?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Norwich\, United Kingdom
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ual-storytelling-research-fellows-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: UAL AKO Storytelling Institute: Storytelling Research Fel
 lows 2026 (six posts)
DESCRIPTION:The UAL AKO Storytelling Institute\, with the Power of Pop Fund
 \, is recruiting six Storytelling Research Fellows to run independent rese
 arch projects on how storytelling in popular culture shapes attitudes\, be
 haviour and public discourse. Fellows work across media including televisi
 on\, film\, gaming\, podcasts and digital culture\, are supported by the I
 nstitute's Head of Research and a peer cohort\, and produce actionable fin
 dings for creative practitioners\, funders and social-impact organisations
 \, presented at a closing public symposium. ELIGIBILITY: experienced resea
 rchers with a PhD or equivalent research expertise gained academically\, i
 ndependently or through professional practice\, with a track record of del
 ivering high-quality research and working with external partners. Practice
 -based and non-academic researchers are explicitly in scope. APPLICATION: 
 CV\, record of publications\, a research proposal of 1\,000 to 1\,200 word
 s\, and a supporting statement including at least one example of a researc
 h project you designed and delivered. Applications via the UAL jobs portal
 \; closes 11 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: University of the Arts Londo
 n (UAL AKO Storytelling Institute)\, with the Power of Pop Fund\nLocation:
  High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)\nAward: GBP 48\,563 per a
 nnum pro rata (around GBP 19\,425 for the part-time fellowship hours)\, pl
 us UAL benefits. No application fee.\nDuration: Nine-month fellowship\, pa
 rt-time\, culminating in a public symposium\n\nMore: https://jobs.arts.ac.
 uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://jobs.arts.ac.uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:paul-mellon-centre-research-project-grants-autumn-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Paul Mellon Centre: Research Project Grants (Autumn 2026 
 round)
DESCRIPTION:The Paul Mellon Centre funds scholarship and dissemination in B
 ritish art\, visual culture and architectural history\, from the medieval 
 period to the present and across the relevant geographical and cultural co
 ntexts. This is the Autumn 2026 round\, the first under the Centre's restr
 uctured funding scheme announced earlier in 2026\, and all grants in the r
 ound close together on Monday 14 September 2026 at 23:59 BST. ELIGIBILITY:
  the Centre's funding is open to INTERNATIONAL applicants\, not just UK-ba
 sed ones (the exceptions are the MA/MPhil Studentship and Doctoral Scholar
 ship\, which are not part of this round). Every applicant must show how th
 e award advances their research on British art\, visual culture and/or arc
 hitecture. SUPPORT: general application guidance\, an updated FAQ and the 
 Our Funding Focus page set out what the Centre looks for\, and there are w
 riteups of previously successful projects. The grants team will take speci
 fic questions or arrange a call at grants@paul-mellon-centre.ac.uk. No app
 lication fee. THIS GRANT: a new award of up to GBP 40\,000 for organisatio
 ns undertaking ambitious and sustained research\, consolidating elements o
 f three previous schemes (the Curatorial Research\, Collaborative and Digi
 tal Project grants). It is designed PRIMARILY to cover STAFF COSTS towards
  facilitating a research project with public-facing outcomes and/or a lega
 cy. The form the outcome takes is deliberately open: an exhibition\, a dig
 ital project\, a catalogue or another means of dissemination all qualify.\
 n\nOrganisation: Paul Mellon Centre for Studies in British Art\nLocation: 
 London-based funder. Applications open internationally\; the research must
  concern British art\, visual culture and/or architectural history.\nAward
 : Up to GBP 40\,000. No application fee.\nDuration: Autumn 2026 round\; ap
 plications close Monday 14 September 2026 at 23:59 BST\n\nMore: https://ww
 w.paul-mellon-centre.ac.uk/about/news/autumn-round-of-funding-opportunitie
 s-open?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.paul-mellon-centre.ac.uk/about/news/autumn-round-of-funding
 -opportunities-open?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
LOCATION:London-based funder. Applications open internationally\; the resea
 rch must concern British art\, visual culture and/or architectural history
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:paul-mellon-centre-author-grants-autumn-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260831T131334Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Paul Mellon Centre: Author Grants (Autumn 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:The Paul Mellon Centre funds scholarship and dissemination in B
 ritish art\, visual culture and architectural history\, from the medieval 
 period to the present and across the relevant geographical and cultural co
 ntexts. This is the Autumn 2026 round\, the first under the Centre's restr
 uctured funding scheme announced earlier in 2026\, and all grants in the r
 ound close together on Monday 14 September 2026 at 23:59 BST. ELIGIBILITY:
  the Centre's funding is open to INTERNATIONAL applicants\, not just UK-ba
 sed ones (the exceptions are the MA/MPhil Studentship and Doctoral Scholar
 ship\, which are not part of this round). Every applicant must show how th
 e award advances their research on British art\, visual culture and/or arc
 hitecture. SUPPORT: general application guidance\, an updated FAQ and the 
 Our Funding Focus page set out what the Centre looks for\, and there are w
 riteups of previously successful projects. The grants team will take speci
 fic questions or arrange a call at grants@paul-mellon-centre.ac.uk. No app
 lication fee. THIS GRANT: up to GBP 6\,000 for INDIVIDUALS disseminating r
 esearch on British art through publication\, covering monographs\, edited 
 collections\, catalogues and articles. It funds the practical side of publ
 ishing\, image copyright fees being the example the Centre gives. This is 
